WEBVTT

00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:04.565
New York keeps a record each time a home
changes hands.

00:00:04.775 --> 00:00:07.768
What can that record tell us about the sale?

00:00:08.800 --> 00:00:11.920
A sale can leave several different records.

00:00:12.130 --> 00:00:16.661
The Department of Finance puts a price and a
date in its sales file.

00:00:16.871 --> 00:00:23.657
The deed records the transfer. A mortgage,
when filed, sits in a separate register
entry.

00:00:23.867 --> 00:00:28.155
The three files have no shared transaction
identifier.

00:00:28.365 --> 00:00:32.330
A researcher has to work out which records
belong together.

00:00:36.200 --> 00:00:44.298
Pablo Loschi linked New York City sales
dated from 2016 through 2025 to deeds and
mortgages.

00:00:44.508 --> 00:00:49.432
The matching rules use the parcel, the
amount and the dates around a sale.

00:00:49.642 --> 00:00:55.850
For condos, the sales file can list the
building lot while the deed records the unit
lot.

00:00:57.500 --> 00:01:05.696
In the four boroughs covered by ACRIS, 97.2
percent of positive-price house sales link
to a deed.

00:01:05.906 --> 00:01:09.732
For condos, the share is 96 percent.

00:01:09.942 --> 00:01:15.629
These are coverage rates. Checking whether
each link is correct takes a separate audit.

00:01:18.750 --> 00:01:23.582
The house paper uses the word cash for a
sale without a matched mortgage.

00:01:23.792 --> 00:01:26.541
Borrowing can still go unobserved.

00:01:26.751 --> 00:01:33.328
The paper compares repeat sales when the
same house switches between the financing
categories.

00:01:39.450 --> 00:01:43.395
The house contrast is 9.25 log points.

00:01:43.605 --> 00:01:49.659
It falls to 4.87 when pairs with a same-
surname buyer and seller are excluded.

00:01:49.869 --> 00:01:53.498
A shared surname flags a possible
relationship.

00:01:54.100 --> 00:02:01.667
Under common controls, the screened house
contrast exceeds the condo contrast by 5.81
log points.

00:02:01.877 --> 00:02:08.067
That comparison uses holds of five years or
more and trims the growth tails within each
property type.

00:02:08.277 --> 00:02:12.535
Records alone give limited evidence on the
value of closing certainty.

00:02:13.900 --> 00:02:16.754
A different question starts at a round
number.

00:02:16.964 --> 00:02:24.002
New York added residential transfer-tax
thresholds in 2019, starting at two million
dollars.

00:02:25.300 --> 00:02:30.328
At two million dollars, the higher buyer
rate applies to the whole price.

00:02:30.538 --> 00:02:36.410
One extra dollar of sale price adds about
five thousand dollars to the buyer tax.

00:02:37.650 --> 00:02:42.170
Sales at exactly two million dollars became
less common.

00:02:42.380 --> 00:02:52.135
Before the reform, the spike at that price
was 7.8 times the surrounding price density.
Afterward it was 2.3 times.

00:02:52.345 --> 00:02:57.524
The share of exact-price sales also fell at
the other thresholds studied.

00:02:58.850 --> 00:03:03.207
More recorded prices appeared just below the
new thresholds.

00:03:03.417 --> 00:03:07.347
Those changes are visible in the
distribution of prices.

00:03:07.557 --> 00:03:12.909
The data leave the paths of individual deals
and who bore the tax unresolved.

00:03:16.950 --> 00:03:20.394
There is another complication in the deed
itself.

00:03:20.604 --> 00:03:26.973
When a lender takes title, the recorded
amount can follow a legal rule tied to the
bid or debt.

00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:33.449
Some lender transfers look like ordinary
sales.

00:03:33.659 --> 00:03:39.469
The study flags 16,655 transfers to lenders.

00:03:39.679 --> 00:03:44.259
About 62 percent pass standard price and
parcel checks.

00:03:44.469 --> 00:03:47.105
They can enter a repeat-sales index.

00:03:48.150 --> 00:03:55.125
Including those amounts lifts the house
index by 3.69 log points in 2010’s third
quarter.

00:03:55.335 --> 00:04:00.418
It lowers the 2011 and 2012 average by 2.28
points.

00:04:00.628 --> 00:04:04.168
The change in the peak-to-trough decline is
imprecise.

00:04:05.200 --> 00:04:11.130
The final paper steps away from deeds to
examine how simulations are evaluated.

00:04:11.340 --> 00:04:18.264
When a policy changes who is still eligible
or waiting, it can change the number used as
a benchmark.

00:04:18.474 --> 00:04:21.930
A worked example shows what that does to a
score.

00:04:23.200 --> 00:04:28.990
In this example, realised value starts at
sixteen. The benchmark is twenty.

00:04:29.200 --> 00:04:32.202
Divide the two and the score is eighty
percent.

00:04:32.412 --> 00:04:39.563
Under the other policy, realised value falls
to eleven. The moving benchmark falls to
eleven as well.

00:04:40.250 --> 00:04:45.995
Now the score reads one hundred percent,
even though realised value has fallen.

00:04:46.205 --> 00:04:50.817
The benchmark changed faster than the
outcome it was used to judge.

00:04:51.027 --> 00:04:53.685
This is a worked simulation example.

00:04:53.895 --> 00:05:00.571
To interpret the score, report the result,
its denominator and the population being
followed.

00:05:03.350 --> 00:05:09.164
What goes into a number matters whether it
comes from a city register or a simulation.

00:05:09.374 --> 00:05:14.113
The public papers show the assumptions and
checks behind each result.

00:05:14.323 --> 00:05:18.854
That helps readers judge which comparisons
the records can support.

00:05:19.900 --> 00:05:27.206
Explore the linked sales, read the research,
and find the code at nychousingdata.com.

00:05:27.416 --> 00:05:32.236
Start with a sale, and see what you can
learn by following its records.
